  "version": "2.1.0",
  "runs": [
    {
      "tool": {
        "driver": {
          "name": "CRSBench-HintGenerator",
          "version": "1.0.0",
          "informationUri": "https://github.com/your-org/crsbench",
          "rules": [
            {
              "id": "CWE-193",
              "name": "CWE-193: Off-by-one Error",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Off-by-one Error"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Specific vulnerability type: Off-by-one Error"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-122",
              "name": "CWE-122: Heap-based Buffer Overflow",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Heap-based Buffer Overflow"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Specific vulnerability type: Heap-based Buffer Overflow"
              }
            },
            {
              "id": "CWE-787",
              "name": "CWE-787: Out-of-bounds Write",
              "shortDescription": {
                "text": "Out-of-bounds Write"
              },
              "fullDescription": {
                "text": "Specific vulnerability type: Out-of-bounds Write"
              }
            }
          ]
        }
      },
      "results": [
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-193",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "CWE-193 - Off-by-one Error\nVulnerability: Heap Buffer Off-by-one Overflow in buffer_write\nDescription: A heap buffer overflow vulnerability exists in the buffer_write function in utils.c\ndue to an off-by-one error in the bounds checking logic. The function uses an\nincorrect comparison operator (>) instead of (>=) when validating the index parameter\nagainst the buffer size. This allows writing one byte beyond the allocated heap buffer.\n\nFor a buffer of size N, valid indices are 0 to N-1, but the current implementation\naccepts index N, causing a write at buf->buf[N] which is one byte past the end of\nthe allocated region. This vulnerability is triggered in fd-exhaustion-harness.c when\nspecific arithmetic constraints are met and buffer_write is called with an index\nequal to the buffer size (300).\n\nThe vulnerability can lead to heap memory corruption, potential control-flow hijacking,\ndenial of service, or corruption of adjacent heap data structures.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
                "artifactLocation": {
                  "uri": "cp-user-stress-test-src/utils.c"
                },
                "region": {
                  "startLine": 24,
                  "endLine": 24,
                  "startColumn": 21,
                  "endColumn": 21
                }
              },
              "logicalLocations": [
                {
                  "name": "buffer_write",
                  "kind": "function"
                }
              ]
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "ruleId": "CWE-122",
          "level": "warning",
          "message": {
            "text": "CWE-122 - Heap-based Buffer Overflow\nVulnerability: Heap Buffer Off-by-one Overflow in buffer_write\nDescription: A heap buffer overflow vulnerability exists in the buffer_write function in utils.c\ndue to an off-by-one error in the bounds checking logic. The function uses an\nincorrect comparison operator (>) instead of (>=) when validating the index parameter\nagainst the buffer size. This allows writing one byte beyond the allocated heap buffer.\n\nFor a buffer of size N, valid indices are 0 to N-1, but the current implementation\naccepts index N, causing a write at buf->buf[N] which is one byte past the end of\nthe allocated region. This vulnerability is triggered in fd-exhaustion-harness.c when\nspecific arithmetic constraints are met and buffer_write is called with an index\nequal to the buffer size (300).\n\nThe vulnerability can lead to heap memory corruption, potential control-flow hijacking,\ndenial of service, or corruption of adjacent heap data structures.\n"
          },
          "locations": [
            {
              "physicalLocation": {
